    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d825">

                <pb facs="0907" n="895" sameAs="frus:frus1944v03/pg895"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 E.W./10–644: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Alexander C. Kirk, Political Adviser, Allied Force
                        Headquarters, to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Caserta</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-10-06T20:00:00+01:00">October 6, 1944—8
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 8:04 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>656. Reference Department’s 171, September 24, 9 p.m.<anchor n="14"
                        xml:id="d825-f1"/> Information has reached AFHQ from British Ambassador in
                    Moscow that Soviet Government has informed him that, although it is still
                    prepared for armistice terms with Hungary to be presented to duly authorized
                    representative, Soviet Government does not consider that General Naday possesses
                    such authority. The Soviet Government also stated that it does not consider it
                    expedient to say anything to Naday about possible help from Soviet armed forces
                    against Nazis.</p>
                <p>Sent Department, repeated to Moscow as 35.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kirk</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d825-f1" n="14" xml:id="d825-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it reported that instructions respecting a Hungarian approach could
                    be sent to SACMED only when the three Allied Governments had reached agreement
                    on action to be taken (740.00119 E.W./9–2444).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
